芝加哥可能会迎来一场血腥的节日。但霍夫曼并没有被吓退，不是吗？一点也没有。他说他想要五天的能量交流，这基本上就是他将要得到的。好吧，纽约人大约在8月20日左右就全都在那里了。我想，那不也是苏联入侵捷克斯洛伐克的日子吗？这件事在这一切的背景下隐隐作响。所以这为他们把芝加哥与布拉格进行比较提供了更多空间。

46
00:22:37.000 --> 00:23:02.000
There's definitely an element of role playing in this, right? The Chicago convention is unfolding at exactly the same point as the Prague Spring. And so a lot of people are saying, oh, these are two sides of the same coin. People are confronting the war makers on both sides of the Iron Curtain. Soviet tanks, Chicago pigs. Exactly right. On the 22nd of August, Hoffman holds a press conference in Chicago and he says, here are our demands, the Yippies demands.
这绝对有一种角色扮演的成分，对吧？芝加哥代表大会展开的时间点与布拉格之春完全吻合。所以很多人都在说，哦，这是同一枚硬币的两面。人们在铁幕的两侧对抗战争制造者。苏联坦克，芝加哥猪猡。完全正确。8月22日，霍夫曼在芝加哥举行了一场新闻发布会，他说：以下是我们的要求，嬉皮士的要求。

47
00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:36.000
They are not unpredictably legalize marijuana. That's a full employment. Turn all the mass media over to the people. End imperialism. Abolish money. Make love, not war. And the last one is blank. He says so people can fill in whatever they want. And the manifesto ends. Political pigs, your days are numbered. We are the second American revolution. We shall win. Yippee. It's not yippee. I suppose it's ambivalent, isn't it? You're thinking yippee in the sort of a slide at a children's party, are you?
它们并不出人意料地包括：合法化大麻。实现充分就业。把所有大众传媒交还给人民。结束帝国主义。废除金钱。做爱，不要作战。最后一个是空白的。他说这样人们就可以填入他们想要的任何东西。宣言结束时的口号是：政治猪猡，你们的日子不多了。我们是第二次美国革命。我们必胜。耶皮。这不是耶皮。我想这是模棱两可的，不是吗？你以为的“耶皮”是在儿童派对上滑一下的那种吗？

48
00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:58.000
Yippee. See, I think of it as more slightly downbeat, yeah, like a bit more stoned, I guess. I don't know. Now the mainstream people, the mob, are very anxious about the hippies being there. They see them as irresponsible and narcissistic. But the mob also are conscious of the potential for violence. So violence, you could say, is built into their protest model too.
耶皮。你看，我觉得它更稍微低落一点，对吧，有点像更嗑大了的那种，我猜。我不知道。现在主流人群，Moab，对嬉皮士的存在感到非常焦虑。他们认为这些人不负责任且自恋。但 Moab 也意识到暴力的潜在可能。所以暴力，可以说是内置于他们的抗议模式中的。

49
00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:26.000
Their slogan, confront the war makers, you know, has the kind of implicit suggestion that stuff might kick off. And their poster is a kind of very aggressive poster. It shows a pig, literally a pig, holding a club, wearing a police helmet, sort of dancing or stamping on a map of Chicago. And I guess that brings us to the other great actors in this drama, which is the Chicago police. You might have the sort of stereotypical vision of the Chicago police.
他们的口号“对抗战争制造者”，你知道的，带有某种暗示事情可能会爆发的含意。他们的海报是一种极具侵略性的海报。它展示了一头猪，字面意义上的一头猪，手里拿着警棍，戴着警帽，在芝加哥地图上跳舞或踩踏。我想这把我们带到了这场戏剧中的另一个伟大演员，也就是芝加哥警察。你可能对芝加哥警察有一种刻板印象。

50
00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:53.000
So they're just enormous burly men eating donuts. Yeah. Who never smile and love cracking heads. And actually that would be a little bit harsh. There's a really good book on this by a guy called David Farber. And I can remember he published it, I think in the late eighties or early nineties. I read many accounts of this convention, but I've never read one in which it had ever occurred to the author to give the story from the point of view of the Chicagoans, of the Chicago
所以他们只是吃着甜甜圈的魁梧壮汉。是的。从不微笑并且热爱爆头。实际上这可能有点过于严苛了。一个叫大卫·法伯的人写了一本关于这方面的非常棒的书。我记得他是...我想是在八十年代末或九十年代初出版的。我读过很多关于这次代表大会的记述，但我从未读过哪一本让作者萌生出从芝加哥人、芝加哥警察的角度来讲述这个故事的想法。

51
00:24:53.000 --> 00:25:24.000
police. So he points out, the Chicago police are not enormously backward. They had been modernized in the previous 10 years by the most innovative police superintendent in the country, it was a guy called O.W. Wilson, and he had promoted and aggressively recruited black officers. So actually, one in four of the Chicago police are black, a better proportion than most other American cities. Yeah, that's not part of the image you have of the events, is it? Never part of the image, but it's important to have that in mind.
所以他指出，芝加哥警察并不是极其落后的。他们在过去10年中被全国最具创新精神的警察局长实现了现代化，那个人叫 O·W·威尔逊，他提拔并大力招募黑人警官。所以实际上，芝加哥警察中四分之一是黑人，这个比例比大多数其他美国城市都要好。是的，这并不是你对这些事件印象的一部分，对吗？从来都不是印象的一部分，但把这一点记在心里很重要。

52
00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:52.000
The morale of the Chicago police is rock bottom. Crime had been growing five times faster than population had, and the threats to policemen's health, life, and so on, are greater than ever before. However, they feel, if you ask a cop, they feel unsupported by the courts, by the political system, and indeed by the public. They feel that their hands tied the courts, by the political system, and indeed by the public. They feel that their hands tied behind their back, all of this kind of thing. I'm not saying I necessarily agree with them. But that's their take.
芝加哥警察的士气降到了谷底。犯罪率的增长速度是人口增长速度的五倍，而对警察健康、生命等的威胁比以往任何时候都要大。然而，他们觉得，如果你问一个警察，他们觉得法庭、政治体制乃至公众都不支持他们。他们觉得自己被法庭、政治体制乃至公众绑住了手脚，所有这些。我并不是说我一定同意他们的看法。但这就是他们的态度。

53
00:25:52.000 --> 00:26:22.000
That's their take. They're conscious, their pay has fallen behind. Farber points out they're paid less than plumbers, less than glaziers, and a lot of other what he calls low status workers. So they're not the elite of the working class by any means. And they feel what they hate more than anything, and I quote, ill-mannered self-righteous college kids whom they believed came from privileged backgrounds protesting in the streets and mixing with and supporting Negroes about whom they knew nothing.
这就是他们的看法。他们意识到，他们的薪水已经落后了。法伯指出，他们的薪酬比水管工低，比玻璃工低，比他所说的许多其他低地位工人还要低。所以他们绝不是工人阶级的精英。他们感到最痛恨、我引述他的话说，是“那些行为粗鲁、自以为是的大学生，他们认为这些人来自特权阶层，在街头抗议，并与他们一无所知的黑人混在一起并支持他们”。

54
00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:51.000
They resented the middle-class demonstrators who talked about equality and freedom, but showed no respect for authority or understanding of those who just wanted to keep the simple things they had worked for so hard. So that is their mindset. Okay. There is class-based tension. Definitely there's class-based tension. Right from the beginning. Huge amounts of class tension. Also, the Chicago police have come in for severe criticism in 68, not for being too harsh,
他们痛恨那些谈论平等和自由却对权威毫无尊重、也不理解那些只想守住自己辛苦换来的简单东西的人的中产阶级示威者。所以这就是他们的心态。好的。这里存在基于阶级的紧张关系。绝对存在基于阶级的紧张关系。从一开始就有。大量的阶级紧张。此外，芝加哥警察在68年受到了严厉批评，不是因为他们太严厉，

55
00:26:51.000 --> 00:27:25.000
but for being too weak. So after Martin Luther King's assassination in April, there had been days of rioting in Chicago. 11 people had been killed and 500 injured and there'd been thousands of arrests. Now the civil rights leaders in the city laid into the police and said, you know, shouldn't have gone in so hard, all of this. Mayor Daley came out and said, I was disappointed the police were so weak. He said, we should be a city of order. Their police chiefs should have told their men that with arsonists, he's horrified by the arson.
而是因为他们太软弱。所以在马丁·路德·金于4月被暗杀后，芝加哥发生了数天的骚乱。11人死亡，500人受伤，数千人被捕。现在该市的民权领袖痛斥警察，说（你知道的）本不该下手这么重，诸如此类。戴利市长出来说：“我对警察如此软弱感到失望。”他说：“我们应该是一个秩序井然的城市。他们的警察局长本该告诉手下，面对纵火犯……”他被纵火吓坏了。

56
00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:57.000
chiefs should have told their men that with arsonists, he's horrified by the arson. And he says arsonists, we should shoot to kill. When people are looting, we shoot to maim. That was his expression. And so with that robust approach to law and order, what is Daly's response to the yippies turning up and Hoffman suggesting that they're planning to put LSD into the Chicago city water supply. Well, you see, this is the thing. Does this go down well? It doesn't go down well at all. Because remember, the Yippies are saying all this publicly. They're not just saying it among themselves. They are saying, for example, we will block all the
局长本该告诉手下，对于纵火犯，他厌恶纵火，并且他说对于纵火犯，我们应该格杀勿论。当人们打砸抢烧时，我们格杀勿论。这是他的原话。因此，在采取这种强硬的法律和秩序方针时，戴利对嬉皮士的出现以及霍夫曼建议他们计划把LSD投入芝加哥城市供水系统的反应是什么？好吧，你看，这就是问题所在。这行得通吗？一点也行得通。因为记住，嬉皮士们是公开这么说的。他们不只是私下说说。例如，他们说，我们会封锁所有的

57
00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:19.000
expressways. We will, as you said, Tom, I mean, this is the story that's repeated in every newspaper, dose the water supply with LSD. We will storm the amphitheatre. We will invade the hotels. And they wanted to paint cars to look like taxis. All of this stuff. And kidnap delegates and dump them. But I mean, you'd have to be a square who's not familiar with Antoine Auteau to take it
高速公路。我们将会，正如你所说，汤姆，我的意思是，这是每家报纸都在报道的故事，把LSD投入供水系统。我们要冲进露天剧场。我们要侵入酒店。他们还想把汽车漆成出租车的样子。所有这些东西。还要绑架代表并把他们扔掉。但是我的意思是，你得是一个不熟悉安托万·奥托（Antoine Auteau）的老土才会把这当真，对吧？我的意思是，只有老土才会这么做。

58
00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:49.000
literally, right? I mean, only a square would do that. I think in a country which has seen years of urban rioting and also has seen a president shot dead five years earlier, the leading civil rights leader, one of the most famous men in the world shot dead that April and a presidential candidate shot dead in June. It is implausible to imagine the city authorities not taking these threats seriously. But Dominic, they are not real threats, are they? I mean, they are ludic threats.
我认为在一个经历过多年城市骚乱的国家里，五年前还看到过一位总统被枪杀，民权运动领袖、世界上最著名的人物之一在那个四月被枪杀，还有一位总统候选人在六月被枪杀。很难想象城市当局不会认真对待这些威胁。但是多米尼克，这些并不是真正的威胁，不是吗？我的意思是，它们是荒诞的威胁。

59
00:28:49.000 --> 00:29:04.000
They are street theatre. They are... I suppose so. I mean, if your car is painted to look like a taxi, you'd probably find it enormously annoying, wouldn't you? Yeah, they're not really planning to do it. They're not really planning to do any of this stuff. I think they are planning to do some of it, actually.
它们是街头戏剧。它们是……我想是吧。我的意思是，如果你的车被漆成出租车的样子，你可能会觉得极其烦人，不是吗？是的，他们并不是真的打算这么做。他们并不是真的打算做所有这些事情。其实我觉得他们确实打算做其中的一部分。

60
00:29:04.000 --> 00:29:17.000
They're planning to do stunts. Because they talk think they are planning to do some of it actually. They're planning to do stunts. Because they talk about setting up mortars and aiming it at the Democrat. Yeah, they're not going to do that. I mean, it's obviously exaggerated. But it's easy for you and me, right? Of course. All these years after the event to sit back and say, well, they are going to do this,
他们打算搞噱头。因为他们谈论……其实我觉得他们打算做其中的一部分。他们打算搞噱头。因为他们谈论设立迫击炮并将其瞄准民主党人。是的，他们不会那样做。我的意思是，这显然是夸张的。但对你我来说很容易，对吧？当然。事发多年后，我们可以坐在这里说，嗯，他们要做这个，

61
00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:42.000
they're not going to do that. How can Daley and his police? But it's another manifestation of this class based tension. Because if you're kind of hip, you've been to an arts college, you've studied theatre, you know that this is obviously a joke. But if you're, as you say, a harassed police chief, of course you're not going to think it's a joke. Of course. So as August approaches, the Yippee supporters are kind of tooling up with flowers
他们不做那个。戴利和他的警察怎么能……但这又是这种基于阶级的紧张关系的一种表现。因为如果你有点时髦，你上过艺术学院，你学过戏剧，你知道这显然是个笑话。但如果你像你所说的那样，是一个饱受折磨的警察局长，你当然不会认为这是个笑话。当然。因此随着八月的临近，嬉皮士的支持者们开始带着鲜花

62
00:29:42.000 --> 00:30:13.000
and books of poetry and jostics or whatever. But Daly's police, they are all issued with riot helmets, they're all given personal tear gas sprays, they're given a shotgun in every car. Frunchans, all the stuff. They are actually expecting an armed rebellion. One of Daly's officials says, we are looking forward to the battle of the century. Oh goodness, I think we should take a break there and when we come back we'll find out if he's right, if we really are facing the battle of the century.
和诗集以及香棒之类的东西做好准备。但是戴利的警察，他们都配备了防暴头盔，都发了个人催泪瓦斯喷雾，每辆车里都配了一把猎枪。警棍，所有这些东西。他们实际上在期待一场武装叛乱。戴利的一位官员说，我们正期待着世纪之战。哦天哪，我想我们应该在那里休息一下，当我们回来时，我们会发现他是对的，我们是否真的面临世纪之战。

63
00:30:13.000 --> 00:31:04.000
A great stillness rose up from the street through all the small noise of clubbing and cries, small sirens. Sigh of loaded arrest vans as off they pulled, shouts of police as they wheeled in larger circles. The intersection clearing further, then further, a stillness rose through the steel and stone of the hotel, congregating in the shocked centers of every room where delegates and their wives and press and campaign workers, innocent until now of the intimate working of social force, look down now into the murderous paradigm of Vietnam there beneath them at this huge intersection of this great city.
一股极大的寂静从街道升起，穿过所有挥舞棍棒的微小噪音、哭喊声和小小的警笛声。满载着被捕者的防暴车发出叹息声，随着它们驶离，警察在迂回盘旋时发出叫喊声。十字路口进一步空旷，然后进一步空旷，寂静穿过酒店的钢铁和石头，聚集在每个房间震惊的中心，代表和他们的妻子、媒体以及竞选工作人员，在此之前对社会力量的亲密运作一无所知，现在向下俯视，看着他们下方那越南的谋杀性范例，在这座伟大城市的这个巨大十字路口。

64
00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:34.000
That was Norman Mailer in Miami and the siege of Chicago. And he's writing about the climax of this battle of the century that one of Daly's officials had promised taking place in the streets of Chicago. So Dominic, before we get to that climax, how does it all play out? How does it begin? So to give people a sense of the geography, the downtown area of Chicago is called The Loop and the main hotels and office blocks are along Michigan Avenue, which runs north to south. So you've got that.
那是诺曼·梅勒在迈阿密和芝加哥围城战中的言论。他写的是戴利的一位官员承诺在芝加哥街头发生的世纪之战的高潮。所以多米尼克，在我们到达那个高潮之前，这一切是如何展开的？它是如何开始的？所以为了让人们对地理有一个概念，芝加哥的市中心被称为环形区（The Loop），主要的酒店和办公大楼都在密歇根大道沿线，这条大道从北向南延伸。所以你有了这个。

65
00:31:34.000 --> 00:32:04.000
Adjoining The Loop next to Lake Michigan is Grant Park. Now further to the north, you have another park, which is called Lincoln Park. And then to the south, there are the great stockyards on which Chicago was built, the biggest in the world. So Norman Mailer in his book, he starts the section about Chicago with a brilliant, brilliant sort of portrait of these stockyards of the blood and the work and all this. And this is quite a distance from the centre, right? From the downtown. Yes. About six miles or something.
紧邻密歇根湖畔环形区的是格兰特公园。再往北，你还有另一个公园，叫做林肯公园。然后向南，是芝加哥建立所在的巨大牲畜屠宰场，是世界上最大的。所以诺曼·梅勒在他的书里，他以一篇精彩绝伦的肖像画般的文章开始了关于芝加哥的部分，描绘了这些牲畜屠宰场的鲜血、工作和所有这一切。这离中心有一段距离，对吧？离市中心。是的。大约六英里左右。

66
00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:31.000
And next to the stockyards is the amphitheatre where the convention will take place. The hotels are in the centre, but the amphitheatre is to the south and the amphitheatre is ringed by a huge barbed wire fence. There are roadblocks, there are armed police, thousands of them on patrol. There's a no fly zone above. There is maximum security. So the Yippies have set up shop in Lincoln Park quite a long way away. And on the Friday before the convention week, they are handing
在屠宰场旁边是举行代表大会的露天剧场。酒店在中心，但露天剧场在南面，露天剧场周围环绕着巨大的铁丝网栅栏。有路障，有武装警察，成千上万的人在巡逻。上面有禁飞区。有最高级别的安全戒备。所以嬉皮士们在离得很远的林肯公园开店了。在代表大会周开始前的星期五，他们正在向人们分发

67
00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:59.000
out maps and flyers to people. There are only a few hundred of them, nowhere near the thousands they had hoped for. I have to say, Tom, their flyers do not suggest a terribly progressive attitude towards gender relations. Chicks who can type and spell, cats who have wheels and want to do a digger trip to feed the masses. Yeah. If you are a chick who can type, I think you might feel a little bit triggered, I believe is the word by that time. Or indeed, if you're a chick at all, whether you can type or not.
地图和传单。他们只有几百人，远没有他们希望的数千人那样多。我必须说，汤姆，他们的传单并不表明他们对性别关系有着非常进步的态度。会打字和拼写的辣妹，有车想去搞一趟“挖掘者”之旅来喂饱大众的猫们。是的。如果你是一个会打字的辣妹，我想你可能会觉得有点被触动，我想那是那个时候的用词。或者实际上，如果你根本就是个辣妹，不管你会不会打字。

68
00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:37.000
So they are handing out maps of the democratic hotels and saying, written on them, break in, break in, break in, security precautions taken by the convention bigwigs are a farce. Again, they think this is very funny. If you're a police agent or something, you read this with a sense of kind of horror. Oh my God, they're going to break into the hotels, right? And so do you find it funny that they're also staging their own nomination battle to elect a president and this president is going to be called Pigasus and he's a pig. And Hoffman, he's brought along a pretty pig, an attractive pig, a sweet little pig.
所以他们分发民主党酒店的地图，上面写着：闯进去，闯进去，闯进去，大会大人物们采取的安全防范措施全都是个笑话。同样，他们认为这非常有趣。如果你是一个警察特工什么的，你读到这个时会有一种恐惧感。哦天哪，他们要闯入酒店了，对吧？那么你觉得有趣的是，他们还在上演他们自己的提名战来选出一个总统，而这个总统将被称为“皮加苏斯”（Pigasus），他是一头猪。而霍夫曼，他带来了一头漂亮的猪，一头有吸引力的猪，一只可爱的小猪。

